Chapter: TruckBody
  cites Gpu chapter DeviceMath
  cites Foreword chapter ListUtils
  cites Safari chapter Geom
  cites Safari chapter Camera
  cites Safari chapter Paint
  cites Safari chapter Trig
  cites Safari chapter World
  cites Safari chapter GuardRail
  cites Safari chapter Render

 The trailer, the cab, the tires, the headlight beams and the brake lights: the
 truck as it is SEEN, where `Safari chapter Truck` is the truck as it MOVES.

 A port of wasm/truck.zig's `drawBody` and the three private drawers under it.
 SPLIT FROM Truck FOR THE SAME REASON CatDraw IS SPLIT FROM Cat, and the split is
 the zig's own seam read twice. Truck's motion half reaches nothing but the world
 and a few of the rider's constants, so World can cite it and every check that
 touches the world pays five constants for it. This half reaches `render.at`, the
 whole chain composition, the camera and the paint wire -- so it cites Render, and
 anything citing it pays for the mapping spine. Keeping them apart is what lets
 TruckCheck stay a nine-case motion oracle while this one grades a draw.

 IT IS THE LAST UNPORTED DRAWING CODE IN THE GAME. What unblocked it was Paint's
 tag 4: the beams and the brake glow are radial-gradient polygons, and until Paint
 modelled that tag there was no honest way to emit them.

 THE ZIG'S OWN COMMENTS SAY THE BEAMS AND THE GLOW ARE DEFERRED, AND THEY ARE
 STALE. drawBody's header and the brake-light block both claim the wedges and the
 halo still need "a gradient/alpha seam tag the polygon-only blitter doesn't have
 yet", and the file's top comment still calls the truck a placeholder blue dot.
 The CODE calls drawWedge and drawBrakeGlow and pushes tag 4 for both. The code is
 the oracle -- the port follows what runs, not what the comment says, and the
 check would have caught the difference either way, because a missing wedge is two
 missing commands and structure is graded exactly.

 EVERY VERTEX GOES THROUGH `at` AND THEN DROPS BY THE GROUND CURVE. That is `p3`
 in the zig, and it is the whole reason the truck reads as sitting on the road
 rather than floating above it: the body is authored in the segment-local frame of
 whichever chain index its centre landed on -- along the road, across from the
 left edge, metres up -- and the fake-horizon curvature is subtracted at the end,
 per vertex, so a long trailer bends with the road it is standing on.

 We say:

Section: Dimensions

 Metres, and all of them mirror truck.ts's buildTruck. The trailer is narrower
 than the four-metre lane so it can round a corner without clipping the rails.

  truck-length : Real
  truck-length = 8.4

  truck-width : Real
  truck-width = 2.4

  truck-height : Real
  truck-height = 3.6

 The cab sticks this far out in front of the trailer, and its roof is this
 fraction of it -- the windshield rakes down over the rest.

  cab-length : Real
  cab-length = 3.5

  cab-roof-frac : Real
  cab-roof-frac = 0.3

  tire-radius : Real
  tire-radius = 0.5

 The trailer floor sits just above the tire tops; the cab floor sits at the tire
 centre line, which is the step the side silhouette walks up.

  trailer-bottom : Real
  trailer-bottom = 2.0 * tire-radius + 0.12

  cab-bottom : Real
  cab-bottom = tire-radius

  tire-pair-gap : Real
  tire-pair-gap = 1.2

  trailer-axle-inset : Real
  trailer-axle-inset = 1.6

  cab-axle-frac : Real
  cab-axle-frac = 0.55

  tire-sides : Integer
  tire-sides = 16

Section: Colours

 Three shades of the same dark blue, which is what makes the prism read as solid
 rather than as a flat silhouette: the sides are darkest, the rear and nose are
 the body colour, the roof is lightest.

  body-color : Integer
  body-color = 1846886

  roof-color : Integer
  roof-color = 3822248

  side-color : Integer
  side-color = 1384784

  brake-color : Integer
  brake-color = 16722456

  tire-color : Integer
  tire-color = 1381658

Section: The Headlight Beams

 Two WEDGES of light thrown forward from the cab-front corners, each a flat 3D
 side-silhouette -- a slit at the truck face fanning open down the road -- filled
 with a radial glow from the lamp. Its 3D angle alone decides how it reads: broad
 in profile, a sliver head-on. Shown only once the sun is behind the mountains.

  headlight-h : Real
  headlight-h = 1.0

  headlight-inset : Real
  headlight-inset = 0.3

  cone-near-half : Real
  cone-near-half = 0.25

  cone-far-center : Real
  cone-far-center = 0.7

  cone-far-half : Real
  cone-far-half = 0.9

  cone-length : Real
  cone-length = 20.4

 THE TWO-COLOUR WORDS ARE 0xAARRGGBB, NOT 0xRRGGBB, and that is what makes a beam
 light rather than a white wedge painted on the road. Warm white 255,248,214 at
 alpha 0.765 in the core, the same white at 0.27 at the edge. Paint's tag 4 is the
 only command besides the beacon that carries alpha at all.

  beam-core : Integer
  beam-core = 3288332502

  beam-edge : Integer
  beam-edge = 1174403286

 The brake-light halo: red 255,80,60 at alpha 0.9 falling to 255,42,24 at zero.
 The edge stop being FULLY TRANSPARENT is the point -- the halo has no rim.

  glow-core : Integer
  glow-core = 3875491900

  glow-edge : Integer
  glow-edge = 16722456

  glow-sides : Integer
  glow-sides = 16

Section: A Body Point

 A point authored in the body's frame -- along the segment, across from its left
 edge, metres up -- mapped into the rider's frame and then lowered onto the curved
 ground. The drop is a POSITIVE distance and the height is `h` minus it, which is
 the same sign convention Render's ground pass states.

  truck-p3 : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real, Real, Real -> Vec3
  truck-p3 (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (along) (x) (h) =
    let r = at segs ch pose d along x
    in Vec3 { right = r.right, forward = r.forward, height = h - ground-drop r.right r.forward }

 The five abscissas and two edges every face is built from, computed once. `a0` is
 the trailer rear -- the face we chase -- and `a2` is the cab nose.

  TruckBox = record {
    a0 : Real,
    a1 : Real,
    a2 : Real,
    a-roof : Real,
    xl : Real,
    xr : Real
  }

  truck-box : Real, Real -> TruckBox
  truck-box (center-along) (hw) =
    let a0 = center-along - truck-length / 2.0
    in let a1 = center-along + truck-length / 2.0
    in TruckBox { a0 = a0, a1 = a1, a2 = a1 + cab-length, a-roof = a1 + cab-length * cab-roof-frac, xl = hw - truck-width / 2.0, xr = hw + truck-width / 2.0 }

Section: The Faces

 A face is a polygon in the rider frame plus the colour it fills with and the mean
 depth it sorts by. The zig recomputes that mean inside its sort's comparison;
 the port stores it, which is the same number and turns a repeated fold into one.

  TruckFace = record {
    color : Integer,
    fwd : Real,
    v : List Vec3
  }

  sum-fwd : List Vec3, Integer -> Real
  sum-fwd (ps) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then 0.0
    else (list-at ps i).forward + sum-fwd ps (i + 1)

  truck-face : Integer, List Vec3 -> TruckFace
  truck-face (color) (ps) =
    TruckFace { color = color, fwd = sum-fwd ps 0 / real-from-int (list-length ps), v = ps }

 EACH SIDE IS ONE SILHOUETTE, not a stack of quads, and walking it is the clearest
 statement of the truck's shape in the whole file: up the rear, along the roof,
 down the windshield and the nose, back along the lower cab floor, up the step to
 the higher trailer floor.

  truck-side : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, TruckBox, Real -> TruckFace
  truck-side (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (bx) (x) =
    let p0 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a0 x trailer-bottom
    in let p1 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a0 x truck-height
    in let p2 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a-roof x truck-height
    in let p3 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a2 x (truck-height / 2.0)
    in let p4 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a2 x cab-bottom
    in let p5 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a1 x cab-bottom
    in let p6 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a1 x trailer-bottom
    in truck-face side-color [p0, p1, p2, p3, p4, p5, p6]

  truck-rear : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, TruckBox -> TruckFace
  truck-rear (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (bx) =
    let p0 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a0 bx.xl trailer-bottom
    in let p1 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a0 bx.xr trailer-bottom
    in let p2 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a0 bx.xr truck-height
    in let p3 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a0 bx.xl truck-height
    in truck-face body-color [p0, p1, p2, p3]

  truck-roof : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, TruckBox -> TruckFace
  truck-roof (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (bx) =
    let p0 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a0 bx.xl truck-height
    in let p1 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a0 bx.xr truck-height
    in let p2 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a-roof bx.xr truck-height
    in let p3 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a-roof bx.xl truck-height
    in truck-face roof-color [p0, p1, p2, p3]

  truck-windshield : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, TruckBox -> TruckFace
  truck-windshield (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (bx) =
    let p0 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a-roof bx.xl truck-height
    in let p1 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a-roof bx.xr truck-height
    in let p2 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a2 bx.xr (truck-height / 2.0)
    in let p3 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a2 bx.xl (truck-height / 2.0)
    in truck-face body-color [p0, p1, p2, p3]

  truck-nose : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, TruckBox -> TruckFace
  truck-nose (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (bx) =
    let p0 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a2 bx.xl (truck-height / 2.0)
    in let p1 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a2 bx.xr (truck-height / 2.0)
    in let p2 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a2 bx.xr cab-bottom
    in let p3 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a2 bx.xl cab-bottom
    in truck-face body-color [p0, p1, p2, p3]

Section: The Tires

 Five axles a side: a tandem pair inset from each trailer end and one under the
 cab. Each tire is a FLAT CIRCLE in its side plane -- no depth, no rim -- so it
 costs one polygon and still occludes correctly, because it joins the face sort.

  truck-axles : TruckBox -> List Real
  truck-axles (bx) =
    let rear = bx.a0 + trailer-axle-inset
    in let front = bx.a1 - trailer-axle-inset
    in let cab = bx.a1 + cab-length * cab-axle-frac
    in [rear - tire-pair-gap / 2.0, rear + tire-pair-gap / 2.0, front - tire-pair-gap / 2.0, front + tire-pair-gap / 2.0, cab]

 The circle is swept in the (along, height) plane at a fixed x, so it lies in the
 side of the body rather than facing the camera. Its centre is one tire radius up,
 which puts the bottom of every tire exactly on the road.

  tire-pt : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real, Real, Integer -> Vec3
  tire-pt (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (ac) (x) (i) =
    let th = real-from-int i / real-from-int tire-sides * two-pi
    in truck-p3 segs ch pose d (ac + tire-radius * r-cos th) x (tire-radius + tire-radius * r-sin th)

  tire-pts : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real, Real, Integer -> List Vec3
  tire-pts (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (ac) (x) (i) =
    if i >= tire-sides then []
    else [tire-pt segs ch pose d ac x i] & tire-pts segs ch pose d ac x (i + 1)

  side-tires : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, List Real, Real, Integer -> List TruckFace
  side-tires (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (axles) (x) (i) =
    if i >= list-length axles then []
    else [truck-face tire-color (tire-pts segs ch pose d (list-at axles i) x 0)] & side-tires segs ch pose d axles x (i + 1)

 SIXTEEN FACES, AND THE ORDER THEY ARE BUILT IN IS PART OF THE ANSWER. The sort
 below is stable, so two faces at the same mean depth stay in build order -- and
 the two sides are at the same depth whenever the truck is square-on to the eye,
 which is most of a straight. Build order is the zig's: both sides, then the rear,
 roof, windshield and nose, then the left tires and the right.

  truck-faces : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, TruckBox -> List TruckFace
  truck-faces (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (bx) =
    let axles = truck-axles bx
    in [truck-side segs ch pose d bx bx.xl, truck-side segs ch pose d bx bx.xr]
       & [truck-rear segs ch pose d bx, truck-roof segs ch pose d bx]
       & [truck-windshield segs ch pose d bx, truck-nose segs ch pose d bx]
       & side-tires segs ch pose d axles bx.xl 0
       & side-tires segs ch pose d axles bx.xr 0

Section: The Painter's Sort

 Farthest first, so the body occludes itself and its own far-side tires. The zig
 insertion-sorts sixteen faces in place and moves a key left only while the
 element before it is STRICTLY nearer, which is stable; this merges instead, for
 the reason Render's own sort gives at length -- an in-place insertion sort
 carried onto persistent lists gains a complexity class. Sixteen faces would
 survive it, but the answer is identical and there is no reason to keep two rules.

 It shares Render's `deeper-than` too, and for the same argument: two depths
 closer than half an f32 ulp are one number in the game and the port must not
 order them. The truck is where that is most likely to matter -- square-on to the
 eye, its two sides sit at exactly the same depth, and which one paints second
 decides nothing about the picture but everything about the command stream.

  face-rest : List TruckFace, Integer -> List TruckFace
  face-rest (ys) (j) =
    if j >= list-length ys then []
    else [list-at ys j] & face-rest ys (j + 1)

  merge-faces : List TruckFace, List TruckFace, Integer, Integer -> List TruckFace
  merge-faces (a) (b) (i) (j) =
    if i >= list-length a then face-rest b j
    else if j >= list-length b then face-rest a i
    else if deeper-than (list-at b j).fwd (list-at a i).fwd
      then [list-at b j] & merge-faces a b i (j + 1)
      else [list-at a i] & merge-faces a b (i + 1) j

  sort-faces : List TruckFace -> List TruckFace
  sort-faces (xs) =
    if list-length xs <= 1 then xs
    else merge-faces (sort-faces (list-take xs (list-length xs / 2))) (sort-faces (list-drop xs (list-length xs / 2))) 0 0

Section: Filling

 Near-clip, project, push -- the same pipeline a rail poly goes through, and it
 borrows GuardRail's `project-all` rather than restating it. push-poly's own
 under-three guard is what drops a polygon the clip reduced to a sliver.

  truck-fill : Integer, List Vec3, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  truck-fill (color) (ps) (cf) (view-w) =
    push-poly color (project-all (clip-near ps near) cf view-w 0)

  draw-faces : List TruckFace, Real, Real, Integer -> List DrawCmd
  draw-faces (fs) (cf) (view-w) (i) =
    if i >= list-length fs then []
    else (truck-fill (list-at fs i).color (list-at fs i).v cf view-w) & draw-faces fs cf view-w (i + 1)

Section: The Wedges And The Halo

 NEITHER GRADIENT SHAPE IS CLIPPED, AND BOTH ARE DROPPED WHOLE INSTEAD. The zig
 returns from drawWedge and from drawBrakeGlow the moment any corner is at or
 behind the near plane, rather than clipping it as a face is clipped. That is not
 an oversight to be improved on in the port: a radial gradient carries a centre
 and a radius in SCREEN space, and clipping the outline would leave the fill
 centred on a lamp that is no longer a corner of it. The truck is always at the
 far end of the chase, so the case is rare -- but it is the case where a clipped
 beam would be visibly wrong rather than merely absent.

  any-behind : List Vec3, Integer -> Boolean
  any-behind (ps) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then False
    else if (list-at ps i).forward <= near then True
    else any-behind ps (i + 1)

  mid-vec : Vec3, Vec3 -> Vec3
  mid-vec (p) (q) =
    Vec3 { right = (p.right + q.right) / 2.0, forward = (p.forward + q.forward) / 2.0, height = (p.height + q.height) / 2.0 }

  pt-dist : ScreenPt, ScreenPt -> Real
  pt-dist (p) (c) =
    real-sqrt ((p.x - c.x) * (p.x - c.x) + (p.y - c.y) * (p.y - c.y))

 The gradient's radius is the FARTHEST corner, so the fill reaches every vertex of
 the shape it is filling and nothing is left flat at the tip.

  max-radius : List ScreenPt, ScreenPt, Integer -> Real
  max-radius (sp) (c) (i) =
    if i >= list-length sp then 0.0
    else real-max (pt-dist (list-at sp i) c) (max-radius sp c (i + 1))

 One wedge from one cab-front corner: near-bottom and near-top at the truck face
 (the slit), then far-top and far-bottom twenty metres down the road (fanned
 open). The lamp is the midpoint of the near edge and the glow is centred there.

  truck-wedge : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, TruckBox, Real, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  truck-wedge (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (bx) (src-x) (cf) (view-w) =
    let p0 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a2 src-x (headlight-h - cone-near-half)
    in let p1 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d bx.a2 src-x (headlight-h + cone-near-half)
    in let p2 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d (bx.a2 + cone-length) src-x (cone-far-center + cone-far-half)
    in let p3 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d (bx.a2 + cone-length) src-x (cone-far-center - cone-far-half)
    in wedge-emit [p0, p1, p2, p3] cf view-w

  wedge-emit : List Vec3, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  wedge-emit (ps) (cf) (view-w) =
    if any-behind ps 0 then [] else wedge-cmd ps cf view-w

  wedge-cmd : List Vec3, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  wedge-cmd (ps) (cf) (view-w) =
    let sp = project-all ps cf view-w 0
    in let lamp = project (mid-vec (list-at ps 0) (list-at ps 1)) cf view-w
    in push-grad-poly beam-core beam-edge lamp.x lamp.y (max-radius sp lamp 0) sp

 Both beams, inset from each cab-front corner. They are pushed BEFORE the body so
 the opaque faces draw over them where they overlap -- mostly they spill onto the
 road ahead, which is what a headlight does.

  truck-wedges : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, TruckBox, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  truck-wedges (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (bx) (cf) (view-w) =
    (truck-wedge segs ch pose d bx (bx.xl + headlight-inset) cf view-w)
    & (truck-wedge segs ch pose d bx (bx.xr - headlight-inset) cf view-w)

 The halo behind a brake-light panel: the panel's screen centre, a radius three
 times its own, and a sixteen-gon laid over it. It is a SCREEN-SPACE circle rather
 than a mapped one -- the light is a glow in the image, not a shape in the world.

  sum-x : List ScreenPt, Integer -> Real
  sum-x (ps) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then 0.0
    else (list-at ps i).x + sum-x ps (i + 1)

  sum-y : List ScreenPt, Integer -> Real
  sum-y (ps) (i) =
    if i >= list-length ps then 0.0
    else (list-at ps i).y + sum-y ps (i + 1)

  glow-pt : Real, Real, Real, Integer -> ScreenPt
  glow-pt (cx) (cy) (rad) (i) =
    let th = real-from-int i / real-from-int glow-sides * two-pi
    in ScreenPt { x = cx + rad * r-cos th, y = cy + rad * r-sin th }

  glow-circle : Real, Real, Real, Integer -> List ScreenPt
  glow-circle (cx) (cy) (rad) (i) =
    if i >= glow-sides then []
    else [glow-pt cx cy rad i] & glow-circle cx cy rad (i + 1)

  brake-glow : List Vec3, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  brake-glow (panel) (cf) (view-w) =
    if any-behind panel 0 then [] else glow-cmd panel cf view-w

  glow-cmd : List Vec3, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  glow-cmd (panel) (cf) (view-w) =
    let sp = project-all panel cf view-w 0
    in let cx = sum-x sp 0 / 4.0
    in let cy = sum-y sp 0 / 4.0
    in let rad = max-radius sp (ScreenPt { x = cx, y = cy }) 0 * 3.2
    in push-grad-poly glow-core glow-edge cx cy rad (glow-circle cx cy rad 0)

Section: The Brake Lights

 Two panels low on the trailer rear, drawn AFTER the body so they land on the face
 we chase rather than being sorted behind it. Each is a soft halo first and the
 bright core on top. They show only while the truck is ACTUALLY slowing, which is
 the flag Truck's motion half computes against the previous speed.

  brake-light : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real, Real, Real, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  brake-light (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (a0) (x0) (x1) (cf) (view-w) =
    let bl = trailer-bottom + 0.20 * (truck-height - trailer-bottom)
    in let bh = trailer-bottom + 0.50 * (truck-height - trailer-bottom)
    in let q0 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d a0 x0 bl
    in let q1 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d a0 x1 bl
    in let q2 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d a0 x1 bh
    in let q3 = truck-p3 segs ch pose d a0 x0 bh
    in emit-brake [q0, q1, q2, q3] cf view-w

  emit-brake : List Vec3, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  emit-brake (panel) (cf) (view-w) =
    (brake-glow panel cf view-w) & (truck-fill brake-color panel cf view-w)

  brake-lights : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, TruckBox, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  brake-lights (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (bx) (cf) (view-w) =
    (brake-light segs ch pose d bx.a0 (bx.xl + 0.10 * truck-width) (bx.xl + 0.36 * truck-width) cf view-w)
    & (brake-light segs ch pose d bx.a0 (bx.xr - 0.36 * truck-width) (bx.xr - 0.10 * truck-width) cf view-w)

Section: The Whole Truck

 Beams under, body over, lights on top. `d` is the chain index Render's truck-at
 found the centre on, `center-along` is how far along that segment it sits, and
 `hw` is that segment's half width -- the centre line the box is built around.

  truck-draw-body : List Segment, List Integer, Pose, Integer, Real, Real, Boolean, Boolean, Real, Real -> List DrawCmd
  truck-draw-body (segs) (ch) (pose) (d) (center-along) (hw) (braking) (headlights) (cf) (view-w) =
    let bx = truck-box center-along hw
    in let beams = if headlights then truck-wedges segs ch pose d bx cf view-w else []
    in let body = draw-faces (sort-faces (truck-faces segs ch pose d bx)) cf view-w 0
    in let lights = if braking then brake-lights segs ch pose d bx cf view-w else []
    in beams & body & lights
